A way of quantifying hot shortness is based on theoretical and/or empirical observations, utilizing a Cu eq  to account for the cumulative effect of tramp elements. In this context, elements that decrease or increase the solubility of Cu or form low-melting phases are weighted with specific coefficients and signs. The main advantage of this approach is that it consolidates multiple individual variables into a single reference value, greatly simplifying comparative analysis. Different formulations for Cu eq  have evolved over the decades and they were summarized in detail by Leroy  et al . [ 22 ]  and Kapoor  et al . [ 27 ]  The coefficient of Ni in these formulations ranges from about 0.4 to 1 and, depending on the literature, Cu eq  contents of 0.2 to 0.4 wt pct are considered critical for surface defect formation. [ 3 , 6 , 7 ]  As outlined by Kappor  et al . [ 27 ]  different formulations lead to different results and especially those that do not take Ni into account are likely to have higher values, which may have a decisive influence on the establishment of critical parameters for processing. One of the most widely recognized formulations was proposed by Melford, [ 7 ]  focusing on thermodynamics. This was subsequently refined by Wolf and Schwabe, [ 6 ]  based on practical process observations, leading to the expression presented in Eq. [ 1 ]. The presentation and interpretation of the results in this study will focus on the Cu eq  after Wolf and Schwabe, [ 6 ]  as it directly accounts for the positive effect of Ni, the negative effect of Sn and in addition the pre-coefficient of Sn also includes effects of As and Sb, whereas the simple approach of the Ni/Cu ratio excludes the correlation with Sn (+ Sb and As). However, the relevance of S in this equation must be evaluated with greater caution today. According to the reasoning of Wolf and Schwabe, [ 6 ]  the presence of S leads to the effective binding of Cu, reducing the potential amount of Cu-rich phase and therefore reducing the risk of hot shortness. In recent decades, however, S contents have decreased significantly due to advancements in secondary metallurgy and increasingly stringent product requirements. Consequently, this mechanism, its influence on hot shortness and the occurrence in the formula may be negligible today. 1 wt pct Cu eq = wt pct Cu + 10 wt pct Sn - wt pct Ni - 2 wt pct S \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
